## The following text has been annotated for parts of speech by students at NTU Singapore as part of a class project in AY 2025-26.
## Verses are extracted from the taggedPBC. Annotations have been developed with a combination of manual and automated approaches, with reference to existing literature and (where possible) native speakers.
## ISO 639-3: blt, Language: Tai Dam, Number of verses: 21
## References: Baccam, D., & Brase, J. L. (1980). English - Thai Dam language lessons (2nd ed). Summer Institute of Linguistics. ; Burusphat, S. (2016). A discourse analysis of the Tai Dam chronicle. Journal of the Southeast Asian Linguistics Society, 9, 30-47. ; Gedney, W. J. (1994). William J. Gedney’s Southwestern Tai dialects: Glossaries, texts, and translations (T. J. Hudak, Ed.). The University of Michigan, Center for South and Southeast Asian Studies. ; Fippinger, J., & Fippinger, D. (1970). Black Tai phonemes, with reference to White Tai. Anthropological Linguistics, 12(3), 83-97. https://www.jstor.org/stable/30029082 ; Fippinger, J. W. (1975a). Black Tai sentence types: A generative semantic approach. In J. G. Harris & J. R. Chamberlain (Eds.), Studies in Tai linguistics in honor of William J. Gedney, pp. 130-169. Central Institute of English language. ; Fippinger, J. W. (1975b). Vietnam word list. Retrieved March 10, 2026, from: https://www.sil.org/system/files/reapdata/14/45/21/144521033713771538387773416256730862181/Vietnam_Word_List_Tai_Dam.pdf ; Hudak, T. J. (2008). William J. Gedney’s comparative Tai source book. University of Hawai‘i Press. https://www.jstor.org/stable/20532978 ; Li, F.-K. (1959). Classification by vocabulary: Tai dialects. Anthropological Linguistics, 1(2), 15-21. https://www.jstor.org/stable/30022180 ; Pittayaporn, P. (2021). Typological profile of Kra-Dai languages. In P. Sidwell & M. Jenny (Eds.), The languages and linguistics of Mainland Southeast Asia: A comprehensive guide (pp. 433-468). De Gruyter Mouton. https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110558142-021 ; Smyth, D. (2025). Thai: An essential grammar (3rd ed.). Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003585848 ; Sriwirote, P., Leong, W. Q., Polpanumas, C., Thanyawong, S., Tjhi, W. C., Aroonmanakun, W., & Rutherford, A. T. (2025). The Thai Universal Dependency Treebank. Transactions of the Association for Computational Linguistics, 13, 376-391. https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00745 ; Vitrano-Wilson, S. (2018). Tai Dam orthographies: Multigraphia, mismatching tones, and mutual borrowing of tone marking devices among three scripts. Written Language & Literacy, 21(2). https://doi.org/10.1075/wll.00015.vit
